// Sources: Managing Successful Projects with PRINCE2 (2023), section numbers as printed in the book; PRINCE2 7 Foundation trainer slide deck (2023), PDF page numbers.
window.BOOKCARDS=[
// ===== Syllabus gaps (Official Book) =====
{l:"p2a",src:"Official Book 3.1",card:{h:"Organizational and project ecosystems (definitions)",d:[["Organizational ecosystem","The internal elements of an organization (including staff, board, owners, and other stakeholders) together with the organization's external relationships such as customers, partners, suppliers, regulators, and competitors."],["Project ecosystem","Those elements of the business involved in or directly impacted by the project and the associated users and suppliers."]],p:["In PRINCE2, 'people' covers those who are working on a project and the relationships between them, as well as those impacted by a project.","Decisions should be made close to where the relevant knowledge resides. They should be escalated only if there is a need to consider the impact on other parts of the project ecosystem, the organizational ecosystem, or to manage wider political implications."],n:"Syllabus 3.1 asks you to define both ecosystems. The course slide only shows Figure 3.1."}},
{l:"p3c",src:"Official Book 6.2.1, 6.2.3, 6.2.4.4",card:{h:"Project board and project team (definitions)",d:[["Project board","Accountable to the business for the success of the project and has the authority to direct the project within the remit set by the business."],["Project team","PRINCE2 uses the term project team to cover all people required to allocate their time to the project."]],li:["The project management team structure is composed of the project board, project manager, team managers, and project assurance and project support roles (6.2.3).","Roles can be shared or combined, within certain limits, as long as the responsibilities are fully allocated (6.2.3).","Every PRINCE2 project must have a project board comprising the project executive, senior user, and senior supplier roles (6.2.4.4).","On smaller and less complex projects, the project executive role can be combined with the senior user or the senior supplier role.","Combining the senior user and senior supplier roles is not recommended: it avoids conflicts of interest in decision-making and keeps the user and supplier perspectives adequately represented."],n:"Syllabus 4.2.4 asks you to define project board and project team."}},
{l:"p5a",src:"Official Book 5.1",card:{h:"Desirable, viable and achievable",p:["All projects must have a business justification, usually documented in a business case. The business case establishes not only the reason for the project but also confirms whether the project is:"],li:["desirable: the balance of costs, benefits, and risks","viable: able to deliver the products","achievable: whether use of the products is likely to result in envisaged outcomes and resulting benefits"],n:"Business justification is not only about costs but also about understanding the value of a project. It is established at the start through the business case, kept under regular review, and if it ceases to be valid the project should be stopped or changed (5.1)."}},
{l:"p5b",src:"Official Book 5.3.1.1-5.3.1.4",card:{h:"Business case technique: the four steps in detail",tb:{head:["Step","What the Official Book says"],rows:[["Develop","The project mandate is used to develop the outline business case, part of the project brief, which the project board approves when authorizing initiation. It is refined into the full business case in 'initiating a project' and approved when the board authorizes the project. Three basic business options: do nothing differently, do the minimum, do more than the minimum. 'Do nothing differently' is always the first option and the basis for quantifying the others."],["Check","Continually assessed for desirability, viability, and achievability. As a minimum the project board checks it at the end of 'starting up a project', at the end of 'initiating a project', at the end of each stage, and when assessing an exception plan. The project manager also checks it when assessing progress, risks, and issues, during the final stage as part of 'closing a project', and when consulting stakeholders about changed goals."],["Maintain","At the end of each stage the project manager updates it with progress data (products delivered, costs, benefits realized) and the latest forecasts, under version control so earlier versions can be compared."],["Confirm","The business reviews it in post-project benefits reviews. During the project, benefits reviews at stage boundaries confirm that benefits forecast within the project are on track. The senior user, who specifies the benefits, is accountable for confirming that they are realized, which may extend beyond the life of the project."]]},n:"Syllabus 4.1.6 covers 5.3.1 including subsections. An alternative procedure can be used, documented as a tailoring decision in the PID."}},
{l:"p5b",src:"Official Book 5.7, Table 5.2",card:{h:"Key relationships with principles: business case (Table 5.2)",tb:{head:["Principle","Achieved by","Resulting in"],rows:[["Ensure continued business justification","creating and maintaining a business case for the project to assess whether it is (and remains) desirable, viable, and achievable","confidence that the investment is worthwhile"],["Learn from experience","using lessons to inform business justification","confidence that the business case is developed from previous experiences"],["Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships","clarifying the responsibilities for developing and maintaining the business case and benefits management throughout the project","clear understanding of expectations for business case and benefits management"],["Manage by stages","ensuring decisions made at stage boundaries are checked for business justification","confidence that the investment remains justified through the project"],["Manage by exception","measuring the impact of issues and risks to the business case and escalating when these are forecast to exceed tolerances","clear understanding of potential impacts on the business case to foster effective decision-making"],["Focus on products","ensuring that the products produced can lead to the required outcomes and benefits","project benefits that are achievable"],["Tailor to suit the project","ensuring that the formality and level of control for business case development, approval, and review are appropriate for the type, size, and complexity of the project","governance of the business case that is fit for purpose"]]},n:"Complete table (syllabus 4.1.2). The course slide shows only three examples."}},
{l:"p3d",src:"Official Book 6.7, Table 6.5",card:{h:"Key relationships with principles: organizing (Table 6.5)",tb:{head:["Principle","Achieved by","Resulting in"],rows:[["Ensure continued business justification","assigning someone from the business to the project executive role who represents the business viewpoint and has sufficient authority and availability to fulfil the role and be accountable for the project","the project is able to adapt to changing business needs, and appropriate decisions are made that align with the business case"],["Learn from experience","using lessons to inform the project management team structure, work breakdown structure, and a healthy project ecosystem","the right people are in the right roles at the right time to deliver the project"],["Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships","developing an explicit project management team structure that ensures everyone involved is fully aware of their responsibilities and relationships","there is no duplication of or gap in responsibilities; there are positive relationships across the project ecosystem and between the project and those impacted by the project"],["Manage by stages","assessing, and where necessary, adapting the project management team structure, role descriptions, commercial management approach, communication management approach, and change management approach at stage boundaries","the project can evolve with changing needs, and appropriate sets of skills and abilities are deployed at each stage of the project"],["Manage by exception","empowering those best placed to make decisions to do so at the appropriate point","there is facilitation of effective and timely decision-making, increased accountability, and ownership of actions and decisions"],["Focus on products","ensuring those who will use the project product are represented on the project management team, and there is an understanding of the change management required for their successful deployment","products are more readily accepted by users and brought into operational use, delivering expected benefits"],["Tailor to suit the project","creating a project management team that is appropriate to the needs of the project and the capability of the organizations and people involved","an effective team structure is created that is appropriately sized and has the necessary responsibilities, skills, and capabilities required to deliver the project"]]},n:"Complete table (syllabus 4.2.2). The course slide shows only three examples."}},
{l:"p6b",src:"Official Book 7.7, Table 7.2",card:{h:"Key relationships with principles: plans (Table 7.2)",tb:{head:["Principle","Achieved by","Resulting in"],rows:[["Ensure continued business justification","aligning the plan's performance targets to the business case objectives and providing estimates upon which the project board can confirm whether the project is viable","ensuring plans remain aligned with the overall business strategy and can be delivered within the defined tolerances"],["Learn from experience","using lessons to inform planning, including those lessons from prior projects or from completed work within the project (for example, lessons from the current stage being applied in the next stage); sharing planning lessons for future projects","improved capability of the project team to deliver the plan and of the organizations involved to plan similar projects in the future through the sharing of data and lessons"],["Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships","establishing the roles and responsibilities specific to a plan; organizing the project through work packages and teams based on organizational factors arising from the project context and delivery method","clear accountability for plan performance and results, enabling efficient decision-making"],["Manage by stages","breaking the project lifecycle into stages as a means of accommodating planning horizons and aligning stages to key decision points as required","project stages and tolerances planned in a manner appropriate to both the business needs and realistic estimates of resources, schedule, risks, and issues"],["Manage by exception","defining levels of plan (project, stage, and team) and establishing the targets and tolerances for them","efficient decision-making and escalation appropriate to the level of plan (project, stage, or team)"],["Focus on products","basing all plans and planning activities on identifying the required products and the most effective way to deliver them","effective communication with stakeholders and avoidance of unnecessary work and products"],["Tailor to suit the project","requiring only planning activities and plans appropriate to the project approach and context","the right balance between project management, delivery activities, and resources"]]},n:"Complete table (syllabus 4.3.2). The course slide shows only three examples."}},
{l:"p7b",src:"Official Book 8.7, Table 8.3",card:{h:"Key relationships with principles: quality (Table 8.3)",tb:{head:["Principle","Achieved by","Resulting in"],rows:[["Ensure continued business justification","developing a quality management approach that designs and delivers products that meet quality specifications required of the business case","ensuring the project products achieve the desired outcomes and remain aligned with overall business strategy"],["Learn from experience","incorporating lessons from quality control activities into quality planning for subsequent stages","recurring elicitation and reporting of lessons learned to improve the quality management approach for the project and to improve organizational capabilities for future projects"],["Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships","establishing the roles and respective responsibilities for quality within the project organization","clear accountability for quality planning, quality control, and quality assurance"],["Manage by stages","aligning quality controls and techniques with stages and stage boundary controls","early identification of quality issues and avoiding the cost and delay of rework"],["Manage by exception","establishing the quality tolerances approved by the project board in the project initiation documentation","efficient means for the project manager to take decisions and report exceptions"],["Focus on products","basing all quality planning and control activities on the project products","effective communication with stakeholders and avoidance of conflicts over user quality expectations"],["Tailor to suit the project","requiring only those quality activities that are appropriate to the delivery method and product characteristics","the right balance between project quality, delivery activities, and resources"]]},n:"Complete table (syllabus 4.4.2). The course slide shows only three examples."}},
{l:"p8c",src:"Official Book 9.7, Table 9.4",card:{h:"Key relationships with principles: risk (Table 9.4)",tb:{head:["Principle","Achieved by","Resulting in"],rows:[["Ensure continued business justification","assessing whether identified risks have a material impact on the business case and hence business justification","increased confidence that the investment is worthwhile and has acceptable levels of risk"],["Learn from experience","using lessons to inform risk identification and management","confidence that responses to risks are based on effective foundations developed from previous experience"],["Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships","clarifying the responsibilities for identifying, managing, and reporting on risks through the organizational layers of the project","clear understanding of expectations relating to risk management responsibilities"],["Manage by stages","ensuring decisions made at stage boundaries are informed by risks, and de-risking the project investment by using stages to formalize 'stop-go' decisions at critical points in the project lifecycle","confidence that the project investment continues to align with business objectives and the level of risk remains acceptable through the project"],["Manage by exception","empowering those most able to own risks and action the risk responses and ensuring right risks and actions are allocated to them and escalating risks that are forecast to exceed tolerances","confidence that the threats and opportunities facing the project are escalated and managed at the appropriate level"],["Focus on products","understanding risks associated with defining, developing, and delivering both specialist and management products","clear understanding of the threats and opportunities relating to developing the products in the project"],["Tailor to suit the project","ensuring that risk management approach and associated procedures are appropriate for the type, size, and complexity of the project","clearly defined, efficient, and effective management of the threats and opportunities that align to relevant organizational standards"]]},n:"Complete table (syllabus 4.5.2). The course slide shows only three examples."}},
{l:"p11b",src:"Official Book 10.7, Table 10.3",card:{h:"Key relationships with principles: issues (Table 10.3)",tb:{head:["Principle","Achieved by","Resulting in"],rows:[["Ensure continued business justification","developing an issue management approach that ensures issues and changes are assessed in terms of their impacts on the business case and the justification for the project","issues and changes being addressed and the project remaining aligned with the overall business strategy"],["Learn from experience","using issue management as a means to capture the lessons learned throughout the project lifecycle and using the lessons to inform responses to issues","ensuring that the project products and delivery activities can continuously evolve and improve in a controlled manner in response to feedback"],["Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships","establishing the roles and respective responsibilities for issue management and change control within the project organization","clear accountability for issue management and changes to the project baseline"],["Manage by stages","enabling issues and changes to be identified and reviewed within stages and at stage boundaries","early identification of issues and avoiding the cost of uncontrolled changes"],["Manage by exception","establishing the authority and procedure for issue management","efficient means for the project manager to manage issues, take decisions, and report exceptions within the authority delegated by the project board"],["Focus on products","linking issues and requested changes to the project baseline","ensuring traceability of changes and clear linkage to products"],["Tailor to suit the project","requiring only the level of change control appropriate to the importance and complexity of the project, the chosen delivery method, and product characteristics","the right balance between control and responsiveness"]]},n:"Answers for the 'Relationships with principles' exercise (syllabus 4.6.2). The book prints 'Learn by experience' and 'Tailor to suit the project context' in this table; the standard principle names are used here."}},
{l:"p12b",src:"Official Book 11.7, Table 11.4",card:{h:"Key relationships with principles: progress (Table 11.4)",tb:{head:["Principle","Achieved by","Resulting in"],rows:[["Ensure continued business justification","checking viability of the business case when progress is reviewed periodically at stage boundaries and for exceptions","more appropriate decisions on the ongoing viability of the project as it progresses"],["Learn from experience","identifying lessons from the output of progress management; applying forecasting techniques based on progress management","lessons being applied to the project or other projects to avoid unnecessary issues or risks in the future; more accurate predictions or estimates about the future of the ongoing project"],["Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships","clarity on reporting requirements and responsibilities for progress management","more timely and effective decisions being made on verified information"],["Manage by stages","dividing the project into stages and authorizing the project one stage at a time; evaluating project progress in accordance with the planned stages","the project being delivered in more manageable chunks, as well as exercising more control over the project resources; more focus when applying the progress management technique"],["Manage by exception","setting tolerances and managing by exception against those tolerances; delegating authority from one level of management to the level below","more event-driven control and raising exceptions to the next level of management when a tolerance is forecast to be exceeded; decisions being made by correct level of management for the project, stage, or work package"],["Focus on products","knowing the status of each product and the project product during delivery","the progress of the project, stage, or work package being aligned with product delivery rather than just time completed"],["Tailor to suit the project","defining the approach to controlling progress; setting tolerances and controls (event-driven and time-driven) appropriate to the risk, complexity, and size of the project at project, stage, and work package levels","an understanding by project team as to how the project will be monitored and controlled; the right level of progress management, monitoring, and control commensurate with the risk, complexity, and size of the project"]]},n:"Complete table (syllabus 4.7.2). The course slide shows only two examples."}},
{l:"p6a",src:"Official Book 7.2.1, 7.2.4",card:{h:"Planning horizon and tolerances in planning",li:["Plans are always based on estimates, which become more uncertain with the length of the project, the type of products or delivery model, and how complex or dynamic the environment is. Plans should only cover the period that can be planned with reasonable confidence: the planning horizon.","A long planning horizon gives a high-level plan (project plan). A shorter planning horizon allows more detail (stage plan). Plans that extend beyond the planning horizon introduce their own risks.","The manage by stages principle keeps plans within reasonable planning horizons: a high-level project plan plus a detailed stage plan for the current stage.","Projects are driven by constraints. A schedule-driven project must be completed by a certain date. A resource-driven project must be delivered without resources beyond those authorized in the PID. Effective planning means understanding which constraints take precedence.","A schedule-driven project has a narrow time tolerance, possibly zero. A project with a fixed budget has a narrow cost tolerance, possibly zero. Scope tolerance example: some tolerance for initial support and user training after acceptance.","How wide or narrow each tolerance is set for each level of plan shows which constraints take precedence."],d:[["Time tolerance","The permissible deviation in a plan's time that is allowed before the deviation needs to be escalated to the next level of management."],["Cost tolerance","The permissible deviation in a plan's cost that is allowed before it needs to be escalated to the next level of management."],["Scope tolerance","The permissible deviation in a plan's scope that is allowed before it needs to be escalated to the next level of management."]],n:"Syllabus 4.3.5: the planning horizon (7.2.1) and using tolerances to manage constraints (7.2.4)."}},
{l:"p7b",src:"Official Book 8.2.1.1-8.2.1.6",card:{h:"Quality planning in detail",li:["User's quality expectations: documented in the project product description (part of the project brief) in 'starting up a project'. It should identify the major products, user quality expectations and acceptance criteria, the standards and procedures to apply (including whose quality management systems are used), useful measurements, and quality tolerances for the acceptance criteria.","Quality tolerances: the project manager works with stakeholders and the project board to prioritize acceptance criteria and quality specifications, which are then used to set quality tolerances in line with manage by exception.","Describing products: requirements are captured in a product description when the need for the product is identified (linear-sequential: in initiation and at stage boundaries; iterative-incremental: evolving in parallel with development). Operating and maintenance needs are included as quality specifications.","When the project board approves a product description, it is recorded in the product register and becomes part of the project baseline. It can then only be changed in a controlled manner.","Product sustainability: sustainability requirements go into product descriptions as quality specifications or acceptance criteria.","Quality responsibilities, specified in the product description: producer (develops the product), reviewer (assesses whether it meets its quality or acceptance criteria, always independent of the producer), acceptance authority (decides if it is acceptable; can be the project board, which may delegate it, stated in the quality management approach).","Quality in subordinate plans: quality control events may be detailed in stage and team plans, but without new requirements that cannot be traced back to the product descriptions. Baseline changes go through the issue management approach."],d:[["Quality tolerance","The permissible deviation in a product's quality that is allowed before the deviation needs to be escalated to the next level of management."]],n:"Syllabus 4.4.5: quality planning (8.2.1, including subsections)."}},
{l:"p7b",src:"Official Book 8.2.2, 8.3.1.2, 8.3.1.3",card:{h:"Controlling quality and accepting products",li:["Quality control begins when a product description is approved and recorded in the product register. A new product with new design or production methods may need a prototype and its production method evaluated first. A mature or off-the-shelf product may only need inspection at the point of delivery.","Quality control activities are recorded in the quality register, which records each activity and its result (typically 'pass' or 'fail') and provides information for end stage reports and the end project report.","If a product fails but is expected to pass, it may be reasonable to repeat the activity. If it cannot pass, the failure is reviewed against quality tolerances and quality specifications. Exceptions to quality tolerances and off-specifications are handled with the issue management technique.","Lessons identified during the product quality lifecycle are captured in the lessons log.","Accepting products: the individuals or roles responsible for acceptance are identified in the product description. Acceptance usually involves reviewing the quality control information and an independent review against the user's quality expectations and acceptance criteria.","Acceptance of a project product typically transfers ownership or responsibility for the product from the project or supplier to the project board on behalf of the user."],n:"Syllabus 4.4.5 and 4.4.6: quality control (8.2.2) and the quality technique (8.3.1, including subsections)."}},
{l:"p11a",src:"Official Book 10.2.1, 10.2.4",card:{h:"Baselines and delegating authority for changes",li:["Change can only be assessed against the project as understood and approved by the project board. Every product, whether in preparation, in development, or approved, is under change control.","A new version of a product is created each time a change is approved and implemented, so changes can be traced to a decision made by the appropriate authority.","The issue management approach is driven by the products to be delivered (the 'what') and the planned delivery activities (the 'how'), so it is usually prepared after the product descriptions and work package descriptions.","The project management team decides: the level at which products are baselined (generally where a component can be independently released, installed, replaced, or modified), how products and versions are identified, what product information the project log holds, the authorities needed to approve and baseline, and the procedure for issues and changes.","Actual product status is periodically checked against the authorized state, typically at the end of each stage and at the end of the project. The product register records the approval and implementation of all changes to the project baseline.","The project board is the ultimate authority for requests for change and off-specifications but may delegate. Too little delegation slows progress. Too much, especially to many different roles, risks diluting alignment with the business justification.","Few changes expected: authority can stay with the project board. Many changes: the board may delegate some decisions. Most changes are generated at work package level, so enough delegated authority is needed there. Authority can be delegated to several levels, based on parameters in the issue management approach."],n:"Syllabus 4.6.5: baselines (10.2.1) and delegating authority for changes (10.2.4)."}},
{l:"p11b",src:"Official Book 10.3.1.4, Table 10.1",card:{h:"Deciding on changes: choices in response to a change proposal (Table 10.1)",tb:{head:["Type","Response","Considerations"],rows:[["Request for change","Approve","The cost of making the change can be funded by use of the change budget, by reducing the scope of other elements of the project, or by requesting additional funds."],["Request for change","Reject","Will the business justification continue to be satisfied, and will expected benefits still be realized if the change is rejected?"],["Request for change","Ask for an exception plan","Can be appropriate if the problem stems from overly stringent quality specifications or acceptance criteria and could be resolved through changes in the approved tolerances."],["Request for change","Request more information","Effectively defers the decision, but may be appropriate if the impacts of approving or rejecting the request for change are complex."],["Off-specification","Accept","What is the impact on the business justification and expected benefits? The project baseline is updated and the off-specification is noted as a concession."],["Off-specification","Reject","How can the issue be resolved? Via the change budget, via an exception plan, or via a contractual remedy (if a supplier is responsible)."]]},n:"Syllabus 4.6.6: issue technique (10.3.1, including subsections)."}},
{l:"p12b",src:"Official Book 11.2.4, Table 11.2",card:{h:"Reports provided by PRINCE2 (Table 11.2)",tb:{head:["Report","From/to","Type","Use"],rows:[["Checkpoint report","Team manager to project manager","Time-driven","Progress and status update of their team plan"],["Highlight report","Project manager to project board","Time-driven","Progress and status update of the project and stage"],["Lessons report","Depends on the context","Event-driven or time-driven","Detailed lessons review of a specific lesson, a stage, or the overall project"],["Issue report","Depends on the context","Event-driven","Enables a request for change, off-specification, business opportunity, or problem or concern to be formally reviewed and responded to"],["Exception report","Project manager to project board","Event-driven","Reports where tolerances have been or are forecast to be breached for the stage or project and seeks direction from the project board"],["End stage report","Project manager to project board","Event-driven","Reports the performance of the stage and requests approval to proceed to the next stage"],["End project report","Project manager to project board","Event-driven","Reports the performance of the project and any subsequent recommendations, and requests approval to close the project"]]},n:"PRINCE2 does not define the composition, format, or presentation of reports. Reports may be electronic dashboards, with access set in the digital and data management approach, and the product register can give a self-service snapshot of product status (11.2.4)."}},
{l:"p12b",src:"Official Book 11.3.1.1-11.3.1.6",card:{h:"Exception management technique: the six steps in detail",p:["Issues that take a stage outside its tolerances are often encountered at the delivering level. They may also occur at the managing level for issues not driven by delivery problems."],steps:["The team manager forecasts that the work package will go outside one of its tolerances and raises an issue for the project manager. If the project manager can resolve it within stage tolerances, no exception report is needed: the issue is reported in the next highlight report and a note may be made in the lessons log.","If stage (or project) tolerances are forecast to be breached, the project manager creates an exception report with resolution options and a recommendation, and sends it to the project board or project executive.","The project board or executive may reallocate project tolerances, reprioritize requirements (de-scope or re-scope), take more time to consider, request an exception plan, or escalate to the business layer if project level tolerances will be exceeded. The business layer then directs the board.","The project manager ceases the current stage and introduces a stage boundary to create the exception plan and adjust related information in the PID. An end stage report may also be produced if it is useful for the decision.","The project board or executive assesses the exception plan: reject it and request amendments, reject it and direct the project manager to continue with the stage (possibly with minor adjustments), or approve it.","The approved exception plan becomes the new stage plan and the project manager authorizes the next work packages. Aligning the new stage end with the old stage end avoids rescheduling the planned end stage assessments."],n:"Syllabus 4.7.6: progress technique (11.3.1, including subsections). Figure 11.3 shows the same six steps."}},
{l:"p4b",src:"Official Book 15.3",card:{h:"Context of 'initiating a project' (Official Book)",li:["Initiating a project enables the project board, via 'directing a project', to decide whether the project is sufficiently aligned with the business objectives to justify its continuation.","The project manager creates the suite of management products required for the level of control specified by the project board.","The project manager should agree with the project board how the management products will be reviewed and approved: each one as it becomes ready, or the full suite as a set.","If the relationship between the user and the supplier is a commercial one, all initiation activities need further consideration, because the risks, rewards, and reasons for the project differ for the user (as the customer) and the supplier."],n:"Syllabus 5.3: context of 'initiating a project' (15.3)."}},
{l:"p9a",src:"Official Book 16.3",card:{h:"Context of 'controlling a stage' (Official Book)",li:["Describes the project manager's day-to-day management of the stage and is used for each stage. Managing a stage boundary activities occur towards the end of each stage, except the final stage.","Normally first used after the project board authorizes the project, but it can be used during the initiation stage, especially for large or complex projects.","Work package descriptions define, assign, and control the work and set tolerances for team managers. If the project manager is also the team manager, work package descriptions should still be used for the individual team members.","Towards the end of the last stage, 'closing a project' is triggered."],n:"Syllabus 5.3: context of 'controlling a stage' (16.3)."}},
{l:"p9b",src:"Official Book 17.3",card:{h:"Context of 'managing product delivery' (Official Book)",li:["Views the project from the team manager's perspective. 'Controlling a stage' views it from the project manager's perspective.","The team manager: accepts authorized work packages, maintains the development, operations, and maintenance relationships identified in the work package description, creates a team plan (possibly in parallel with the stage plan), develops products using any specified development methods, demonstrates that each product meets its quality criteria, obtains approval from the authorities in the product description, and delivers the products to the project manager.","When external suppliers do not use PRINCE2, managing product delivery provides a statement of the required working relationship between the team manager and the project's PRINCE2 method, as applied by the project manager.","A work package may be part of a contractual agreement, so a team plan can range from a schedule appended to the work package description to a fully formed plan."],n:"Syllabus 5.3: context of 'managing product delivery' (17.3)."}},
{l:"p14a",src:"Official Book 14.3",card:{h:"Context of 'directing a project' (Official Book)",li:["Starts on completion of 'starting up a project', triggered by the request to initiate a project. It covers the activities of the project board, not the project manager's day-to-day activities.","The project board manages by exception: it monitors via reports and controls through a small number of decision points, so no other progress meetings should be needed. Levels of authority and decision-making should be clearly defined and empower the project manager and team managers.","There needs to be a two-way flow of information between the project board and the business layer. How the board acts as a communication channel is documented in the communication management approach.","The board should provide unified direction. If it cannot provide a single view, the risk of failure increases because the project manager may act on contradictory requirements and priorities. In such cases, the project executive makes the decision.","The process lets the board ensure continued business justification without being overburdened by hands-on project management, and avoids micromanaging the project manager."],n:"Syllabus 5.3: context of 'directing a project' (14.3)."}},
{l:"p4a",src:"Official Book 5.5, A11; Trainer deck p.153-154",card:{h:"Project brief: purpose and composition",p:["Purpose: to provide a full and firm foundation for the initiation of the project."],tb:{head:["Element","Typical content"],rows:[["Project definition","Background, project objectives, desired outcomes, project scope and exclusions, constraints and assumptions, project tolerances, users and any other known interested parties"],["Outline business case","Reasons why the project is needed and the business option selected (deck task answer: reasons, benefits, time, cost, major risks)"],["Project product description","User quality expectations and acceptance criteria (deck task answer: purpose, major products, user's quality expectations, acceptance criteria with tolerances, acceptance method and responsibilities)"],["Project approach","The approach to deliver the selected business option (deck task answer: delivery method, for example sequential, hybrid, or agile, and delivery solution, for example in-house or contracted)"],["Project management team structure and role descriptions","Who will work on the project, in what role, and with what scheme of delegation and reporting"],["References","Any associated documents or products"]]},n:"Produced by the project manager and project executive in 'starting up a project', approved by the project board when authorizing initiation, then part of the PID (A11). Includes the answer to the workbook 'Project brief task'."}},
// ===== Trainer deck exercise answers =====
{l:"p12b",src:"Trainer deck p.340-341",card:{h:"Matching terms exercise (answers)",tb:{head:["Definition","Term"],rows:[["A Responsible for setting work package tolerances","6 Project manager"],["B The result of change normally affecting real-world behaviour and/or circumstances","5 Outcome"],["C To list all the products required of a plan and the status of those products","9 Product register"],["D To provide a full and firm foundation for the initiation of the project","10 Project brief"],["E The process that controls the link between the project manager and the team manager","3 Managing product delivery"],["F To report where tolerances have or are forecast to be breached","8 Exception report"],["G Doing the minimum necessary to decide whether it is worthwhile to initiate the project","1 Starting up a project"],["H Accountable for the quality of the products delivered","7 Senior supplier"],["I The measurable improvement resulting from an outcome, perceived as an advantage by the investing organization","2 Benefit"],["J A guiding obligation that ensures effective application and tailoring of PRINCE2 to any project","4 Principle"],["K Maintains information on all the identified threats and opportunities","15 Risk register"],["L A problem, concern, change request, or off-specification","18 Issue"],["M Any individual or group that can affect or be affected by the project","20 Stakeholder"],["N The process that covers the activities of the project board","17 Directing a project"],["O A diagram showing the sequence of production of products and interdependencies","12 Product flow diagram"],["P The single point of accountability for the project","14 Project executive"],["Q People from across the project ecosystem working together to achieve the project's objectives","19 Collaboration"],["R The section of a project that the project manager is managing on behalf of the project board at any one time","11 Stage"],["S To provide a progress and status update of the stage/project at pre-determined intervals","16 Highlight report"],["T Providing services such as administration, planning support, and guidance on project tools","13 Project support"]]},n:"Answer key for the 'Matching terms' worksheet in the learner workbook (Appendix 1). The term numbers match the worksheet."}},
{l:"p14a",src:"Trainer deck p.369",card:{h:"Quiz: processes and activities (answers)",tb:{head:["Activity","Process"],rows:[["1 Establish project controls","Initiating a project"],["2 Execute a work package","Managing product delivery"],["3 Authorize project closure","Directing a project"],["4 Assemble the project brief","Starting up a project"],["5 Report highlights","Controlling a stage"],["6 Prepare exception plan","Managing a stage boundary"],["7 Evaluate stage status","Controlling a stage"],["8 Evaluate the project","Closing a project"],["9 Prepare the outline business case","Starting up a project"],["10 Request next stage","Managing a stage boundary"],["11 Prepare the project plan","Initiating a project"],["12 Prepare premature closure","Closing a project"],["13 Agree tailoring requirements","Initiating a project"],["14 Authorize the project","Directing a project"],["15 Authorize a work package","Controlling a stage"]]},n:"Answer key for the 'Processes and activities' quiz in the learner workbook (Appendix 1). Same activities and numbering as the worksheet."}},
{l:"p2b",src:"Trainer deck p.93; Official Book 3.4",card:{h:"Communication management approach: what to consider",li:["What will be communicated and how this will be tailored to stakeholder groups","Frequency","Tools and techniques","Responsibilities and resources","Standards and guidelines"],p:["Official Book 3.4 high-level content: scope, stakeholder analysis, communications schedule and procedure(s) for each stakeholder group (purpose, frequency, channels/format, messaging), responsibilities, resources, supporting tools and techniques, standards, references."],n:"The 'suggested list of considerations' referred to in the workbook notes for the communication approach activity. The approach is equally about listening as it is about broadcasting (3.4)."}}
];
window.QB=window.QB||[];
QB.push(
// ===== Syllabus gap questions (Official Book) =====
{m:"p2",l:"p2a",bk:1,q:"Which is the PRINCE2 definition of the project ecosystem?",o:["Those elements of the business involved in or directly impacted by the project, and the associated users and suppliers","The internal elements of an organization together with its external relationships such as customers, regulators and competitors","All the people who are required to allocate their time to the project, from the project board to team members","The business, user and supplier interests that the project board represents for the duration of the project"],e:"Official Book 3.1 (Definitions): the project ecosystem is 'those elements of the business involved in or directly impacted by the project and the associated users and suppliers'.",x:["That is the organizational ecosystem (3.1).","That is the project team (6.2.3).","Those are the three project interests (6.2.1)."]},
{m:"p2",l:"p2a",bk:1,q:"A project's regulators and competitors are part of which ecosystem, according to the PRINCE2 definitions?",o:["The organizational ecosystem","The project ecosystem","The project management team","The delivering layer"],e:"Official Book 3.1: the organizational ecosystem is the internal elements of an organization together with its external relationships such as customers, partners, suppliers, regulators, and competitors.",x:["The project ecosystem is the parts of the business involved in or directly impacted by the project, plus its users and suppliers.","Not a team: regulators and competitors are external relationships.","A management level of the project, not an ecosystem."]},
{m:"p3",l:"p3c",bk:1,q:"Which combination of project board roles does the Official Book NOT recommend?",o:["Senior user with senior supplier","Project executive with senior user","Project executive with senior supplier","None: any of the roles may be combined"],e:"Official Book 6.2.4.4: on smaller projects the project executive role can be combined with the senior user or senior supplier role, but combining senior user and senior supplier is not recommended, to avoid conflicts of interest.",x:["Allowed on smaller and less complex projects.","Allowed on smaller and less complex projects.","The senior user and senior supplier combination is not recommended."]},
{m:"p5",l:"p5a",bk:1,q:"In the business case practice, what does it mean for a project to be 'viable'?",o:["The project is able to deliver the products","The balance of costs, benefits and risks is favourable","Use of the products is likely to lead to the expected benefits","The project fits within the business layer's risk appetite"],e:"Official Book 5.1: desirable = the balance of costs, benefits, and risks. Viable = able to deliver the products. Achievable = whether use of the products is likely to result in envisaged outcomes and resulting benefits.",x:["That is 'desirable'.","That is 'achievable'.","Risk appetite is a risk concept, not one of the three tests."]},
{m:"p5",l:"p5b",bk:1,q:"Which business option should always be analysed first in the business case?",o:["Do nothing differently","Do the minimum","Do more than the minimum","The option preferred by the executive"],e:"Official Book 5.3.1.1: the three basic business options are do nothing differently, do the minimum, and do more than the minimum. 'Do nothing differently' should always be the first option and acts as the basis for quantifying the other options.",x:["The second basic option, quantified against 'do nothing differently'.","The third basic option.","Options are compared on value, not on preference."]},
{m:"p5",l:"p5b",bk:1,q:"Who is accountable for confirming that the forecast benefits are realized, possibly after the project has closed?",o:["The senior user","The project executive","The project manager","Project assurance"],e:"Official Book 5.3.1.4: the senior user, who is responsible for specifying the benefits, is also accountable for confirming that the forecast benefits are realized. This may involve a commitment beyond the life of the project.",x:["Accountable for the business case during the project, but benefits confirmation sits with the senior user.","Updates the business case and reports benefits measured during the project.","Assures the board; not accountable for benefits."]},
{m:"p5",l:"p5b",bk:1,q:"In the business case practice, 'measuring the impact of issues and risks to the business case and escalating when these are forecast to exceed tolerances' applies which principle?",o:["Manage by exception","Manage by stages","Ensure continued business justification","Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships"],e:"Official Book Table 5.2: manage by exception is achieved by measuring the impact of issues and risks to the business case and escalating when they are forecast to exceed tolerances.",x:["Manage by stages: checking business justification for decisions at stage boundaries.","Ensure continued business justification: creating and maintaining the business case.","Define roles: clarifying responsibilities for the business case and benefits management."]},
{m:"p3",l:"p3d",bk:1,q:"In the organizing practice, 'empowering those best placed to make decisions to do so at the appropriate point' applies which principle?",o:["Manage by exception","Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships","Tailor to suit the project","Manage by stages"],e:"Official Book Table 6.5: manage by exception is achieved by empowering those best placed to make decisions to do so at the appropriate point, resulting in effective and timely decision-making and increased accountability.",x:["Achieved by developing an explicit project management team structure.","Achieved by creating a project management team appropriate to the project's needs.","Achieved by assessing and adapting the team structure and approaches at stage boundaries."]},
{m:"p6",l:"p6b",bk:1,q:"In the plans practice, 'requiring only planning activities and plans appropriate to the project approach and context' applies which principle?",o:["Tailor to suit the project","Focus on products","Manage by exception","Learn from experience"],e:"Official Book Table 7.2: tailor to suit the project is achieved by requiring only planning activities and plans appropriate to the project approach and context.",x:["Focus on products: basing all plans on identifying the required products.","Manage by exception: defining levels of plan with their targets and tolerances.","Learn from experience: using and sharing planning lessons."]},
{m:"p7",l:"p7b",bk:1,q:"Incorporating lessons from quality control activities into quality planning for subsequent stages applies which principle?",o:["Learn from experience","Manage by stages","Focus on products","Ensure continued business justification"],e:"Official Book Table 8.3: learn from experience is achieved by incorporating lessons from quality control activities into quality planning for subsequent stages.",x:["Manage by stages: aligning quality controls with stages and stage boundary controls.","Focus on products: basing quality planning and control on the project products.","A quality management approach that meets the quality specifications required of the business case."]},
{m:"p8",l:"p8c",bk:1,q:"In the risk practice, understanding the risks of defining, developing, and delivering both specialist and management products applies which principle?",o:["Focus on products","Manage by exception","Learn from experience","Tailor to suit the project"],e:"Official Book Table 9.4: focus on products is achieved by understanding risks associated with defining, developing, and delivering both specialist and management products.",x:["Manage by exception: empowering risk owners and escalating risks forecast to exceed tolerances.","Learn from experience: using lessons to inform risk identification and management.","Tailor: an appropriate risk management approach for the type, size and complexity of the project."]},
{m:"p11",l:"p11b",bk:1,q:"Which principle does the issues practice apply by linking issues and requested changes to the project baseline?",o:["Focus on products","Manage by stages","Manage by exception","Ensure continued business justification"],e:"Official Book Table 10.3: focus on products is achieved by linking issues and requested changes to the project baseline, ensuring traceability of changes and clear linkage to products.",x:["Manage by stages: identifying and reviewing issues within stages and at stage boundaries.","Manage by exception: establishing the authority and procedure for issue management.","Assessing issues and changes for their impact on the business case."]},
{m:"p11",l:"p11b",bk:1,q:"Requiring only the level of change control appropriate to the importance and complexity of the project, the delivery method and product characteristics applies which principle?",o:["Tailor to suit the project","Manage by exception","Focus on products","Define roles, responsibilities, and relationships"],e:"Official Book Table 10.3: tailor to suit the project, resulting in the right balance between control and responsiveness.",x:["Manage by exception: establishing the authority and procedure for issue management.","Focus on products: linking issues and requested changes to the project baseline.","Define roles: responsibilities for issue management and change control."]},
{m:"p12",l:"p12b",bk:1,q:"Which principle does the progress practice apply by identifying lessons from the output of progress management and applying forecasting techniques based on it?",o:["Learn from experience","Manage by exception","Manage by stages","Focus on products"],e:"Official Book Table 11.4: learn from experience, resulting in lessons applied to this or other projects and more accurate predictions about the ongoing project.",x:["Manage by exception: setting tolerances and delegating authority.","Manage by stages: authorizing the project one stage at a time.","Focus on products: knowing the status of each product during delivery."]},
{m:"p6",l:"p6a",bk:1,q:"A project must be completed by a fixed date to meet a new regulation. How should its time tolerance be set?",o:["Narrow, possibly zero","Wide, to absorb any delays","Equal to its cost tolerance","Only at team plan level"],e:"Official Book 7.2.4: the plan for a schedule-driven project will have a narrow time tolerance, possibly set to zero, indicating that any delays would put the project at risk of failure.",x:["A wide time tolerance would suit a project where the date is not the main constraint.","Tolerances are set separately for each aspect, according to which constraints take precedence.","Time tolerance is set at project, stage and team levels."]},
{m:"p7",l:"p7b",bk:1,q:"According to the quality responsibilities in a product description, who must always be independent of the producer?",o:["The reviewer","The acceptance authority","The team manager","Project support"],e:"Official Book 8.2.1.5: reviewers should always be independent of producers to avoid a conflict of interest. The acceptance authority can be the project board, which may delegate it.",x:["Decides whether the product is acceptable; independence from the producer is stated for reviewers.","Often responsible for producing products, so not independent.","Administrative support role; not a quality responsibility in the product description."]},
{m:"p7",l:"p7b",bk:1,q:"When does a product description become part of the project baseline?",o:["When the project board approves it and it is recorded in the product register","When the team manager accepts the work package that it belongs to","When the product first passes a quality control activity such as a test","When the project manager adds the product to the next stage plan"],e:"Official Book 8.2.1.3: when a product description is approved by the project board, it is recorded in the product register and becomes part of the project baseline. It can then be changed only in a controlled manner.",x:["Accepting a work package does not baseline its product descriptions.","Quality control begins after the product description is approved.","Planning the product does not baseline its description."]},
{m:"p7",l:"p7b",bk:1,q:"A product fails a quality control activity and is not expected to pass if it is repeated. What should happen next?",o:["Review it against quality tolerances and specifications, using issue management if needed","Record a concession in the quality register and move on to the next quality activity","Repeat the activity until it passes, as the quality register only accepts a pass","Ask the producer to accept the product on behalf of the user and close the activity"],e:"Official Book 8.3.1.2: if a product cannot pass a quality control activity, the failure must be reviewed against quality tolerances and quality specifications. Exceptions to quality tolerances and off-specifications are addressed using the issue management technique.",x:["A concession is a project board decision on an off-specification, not a quality register entry.","Repeating is reasonable only when the product is expected to pass.","Acceptance is by the authorities named in the product description, never the producer."]},
{m:"p11",l:"p11a",bk:1,q:"Why is the issue management approach usually prepared after the product descriptions and work package descriptions?",o:["It is driven by the products to be delivered and the planned delivery activities","It needs the change budget, which is only known after the stage plans are approved","The project board can only delegate change authority once work packages exist","It replaces the change control requirements written in each work package"],e:"Official Book 10.2.1: the issue management approach is driven by the nature of the products to be delivered (the 'what') and the planned delivery activities (the 'how'), so its preparation usually follows the product descriptions and work package descriptions.",x:["Not the reason given in the book.","Not the reason given in the book.","Work package descriptions contain change control requirements in line with the approach."]},
{m:"p11",l:"p11a",bk:1,q:"According to the Official Book, at which level are most changes generated?",o:["Work package level","Stage level","Project level","Business layer level"],e:"Official Book 10.2.4: in practice, most changes will be generated at the work package level, so sufficient delegated authority is needed to approve changes for work packages without always escalating to the project board.",x:["Not where most changes arise.","Not where most changes arise.","The business layer sets the context; it does not generate most changes."]},
{m:"p11",l:"p11b",bk:1,q:"An off-specification is accepted. According to Table 10.1, what happens next?",o:["The project baseline is updated and the off-specification is noted as a concession","The change is funded from the change budget and logged as a request for change","An exception plan is always requested from the project manager by the board","The decision is deferred until more information about its impact is available"],e:"Official Book Table 10.1: for an off-specification, 'accept' means considering the impact on the business justification and expected benefits. The project baseline is updated and the off-specification is noted as a concession.",x:["That is one way to resolve a rejected off-specification or fund an approved request for change.","An exception plan is one way to resolve a rejected off-specification.","'Request more information' is a response to a request for change."]},
{m:"p11",l:"p11b",bk:1,q:"Which is a way of funding an approved request for change listed in Table 10.1?",o:["Reducing the scope of other elements of the project","Using the risk budget set aside for contingent plans","Moving cost tolerance from the next stage plan","Delaying the next stage boundary by one month"],e:"Official Book Table 10.1: the cost of an approved request for change can be funded by use of the change budget, by reducing the scope of other elements of the project, or by requesting additional funds.",x:["The risk budget funds risk responses, not changes.","Not a funding option in Table 10.1.","Not a funding option in Table 10.1."]},
{m:"p12",l:"p12b",bk:1,q:"Which TWO reports are time-driven according to Table 11.2?\n1. End stage report\n2. Checkpoint report\n3. Exception report\n4. Highlight report",o:["2 and 4","1 and 2","1 and 3","3 and 4"],e:"Official Book Table 11.2: checkpoint and highlight reports are time-driven. The issue, exception, end stage and end project reports are event-driven. The lessons report can be event-driven or time-driven.",x:["The end stage report is event-driven.","Both are event-driven.","The exception report is event-driven."]},
{m:"p12",l:"p12b",bk:1,q:"After receiving an exception report, which is NOT one of the project board's options in the exception management technique?",o:["Instruct the team manager to prepare an exception plan for the stage","Reallocate project tolerances to resolve the breach of stage tolerance","Request an exception plan from the project manager","Escalate to the business layer if project tolerances will be exceeded"],e:"Official Book 11.3.1.3: the board may reallocate project tolerances, reprioritize requirements, take more time, request an exception plan from the project manager, or escalate to the business layer. Exception plans are prepared by the project manager.",x:["An option listed in step 3.","An option listed in step 3.","An option listed in step 3."]},
{m:"p4",l:"p4b",bk:1,q:"During initiation, what should the project manager agree with the project board about the management products being created?",o:["How they will be reviewed and approved: one by one as ready, or as a full set","Which of them can be omitted because the project brief already covers them","Which team managers will produce them under work package descriptions","When the business layer will baseline them under its own change control"],e:"Official Book 15.3: the project manager should have agreed with the project board how they will review and approve the management products, for example each one as it becomes ready or the full suite as a set.",x:["Not stated; the PID builds on the project brief.","Management products are created by the project manager in initiation.","The project board approves them; the project baselines them."]},
{m:"p9",l:"p9a",bk:1,q:"On a small project the project manager also performs the team manager role. How should work be allocated to team members?",o:["Through work package descriptions","Verbally, with no need for work packages","By listing tasks in the stage plan only","By agreeing tasks in checkpoint reports"],e:"Official Book 16.3: if the project manager is fulfilling the team manager role, work package descriptions should still be used to define and control the work of the individual team members.",x:["The book says work package descriptions should still be used.","Work is still defined and controlled through work package descriptions.","Checkpoint reports report progress; they do not allocate work."]},
{m:"p9",l:"p9b",bk:1,q:"A supplier's team does not use PRINCE2. How does the method still control their work?",o:["Managing product delivery states the working relationship that is required","The supplier must adopt PRINCE2 before it can accept any work package","The senior supplier takes over the project manager's role for that work","The project board authorizes that team's work packages directly instead"],e:"Official Book 17.3: when external suppliers do not use PRINCE2, managing product delivery provides a statement of the required working relationship between the team manager and the project's PRINCE2 method.",x:["Not required by the book.","Roles do not change.","Work packages are authorized by the project manager in 'controlling a stage'."]},
{m:"p14",l:"p14a",bk:1,q:"The senior user and senior supplier give the project manager contradictory direction. According to the Official Book, who makes the decision?",o:["The project executive","The project manager","The business layer","Project assurance"],e:"Official Book 14.3: the project board should provide unified direction. If it cannot provide a single view, the risk of failure increases, and in such cases the project executive makes the decision.",x:["The project manager should not act on contradictory requirements.","The business layer is not involved in this case.","Project assurance advises; it does not decide."]},
{m:"p4",l:"p4a",bk:1,q:"Which element of the project brief defines the approach to deliver the selected business option, including the delivery method?",o:["Project approach","Project definition","Outline business case","Project product description"],e:"Official Book A11: the project approach defines the approach to deliver the business option selected from the business case. The trainer deck answer adds delivery method (sequential, hybrid, or agile) and delivery solution (in-house or contracted).",x:["Background, objectives, desired outcomes, scope, constraints, tolerances, users.","Reasons why the project is needed and the business option selected.","Major products, user quality expectations and acceptance criteria."]},
// ===== Trainer deck review question with no v7.2 equivalent (answer per the Official Book; the deck PDF does not mark answers) =====
{m:"p7",l:"p7b",bk:1,q:"Which statement describes project assurance, rather than quality assurance?",o:["It is independent of the project manager but not the project","It monitors the project's quality control measures used to assess the project's products","It is independent of the project team and may be part of the user's quality management system","It describes the documented expectations for the project products"],e:"Trainer deck p.223 quiz. Official Book 8.2.3: project assurance is the responsibility of the project board and is independent of the project manager but not the project. Quality assurance is independent of the project management team, typically performed by a function from the business.",x:["That describes quality assurance, which checks the quality control measures are appropriate.","That describes quality assurance, which is independent of the project team.","That describes the user's quality expectations, not assurance."]}
);

;
